---
title: Theme Configuration Overview
---

The iOS app's visual theme lives in a single JSON file in your local clone: **`VERA/Theme/semantics.json`**. This tutorial covers the two parts of that file you can meaningfully customize — **colors** and **typography sizing** — without touching any Swift source code.

## Where It Lives

{% filetree %}

- VERA/
  - App/
  - Config/
    - app-config.json
  - Scripts/
  - Theme/
    - semantics.json   ← Theme tokens live here
  - VERAApp/
  - VERAConfiguration/
  - VERACommonUI/

{% /filetree %}

## How It's Structured

`semantics.json` nests every token under `themes.vonage`, split into three groups: `colors`, `borderRadius`, and `typography`:

```json
{
  "themes": {
    "vonage": {
      "colors": {
        "light": { "primary": "#9941FF", "background": "#F5F0FD", "...": "..." },
        "dark":  { "primary": "#B27BF2", "background": "#26044D", "...": "..." }
      },
      "borderRadius": {
        "none": 0, "extra-small": 2, "small": 4, "medium": 8, "large": 12, "extra-large": 24
      },
      "typography": {
        "font-family": "Inter",
        "desktop": { "headline": { "font-size": "66px", "line-height": "88px", "font-weight": 500 }, "...": "..." },
        "mobile":  { "headline": { "font-size": "32px", "line-height": "40px", "font-weight": 500 }, "...": "..." }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

- **`colors.light` / `colors.dark`** — Separate color palettes for each mode. Both modes use the same set of semantic token keys. **Editable in this tutorial.**
- **`typography`** — A shared `font-family`, plus separate `desktop` and `mobile` type scales, each with `font-size` and `line-height` per named token (e.g. `headline`, `body-base`). **The sizes are editable in this tutorial.**
- **`borderRadius`** — Six named tokens controlling corner rounding. Present in the file, but out of scope for this tutorial — see the note below.

{% aside type="caution" %}
This tutorial only covers `colors` and the `font-size`/`line-height` values under `typography`. Two things are intentionally out of scope:

- **`borderRadius`** — on iOS, in this exercise, changing these values has no visible effect on the built app, so we won't edit them.
- **`typography.font-family`** — the JSON reports `"Inter"`, but the iOS app actually renders text using the system's native font regardless of this value, so changing it won't change anything you see.
{% /aside %}

## Color Token Reference

Here's the subset of color tokens you'll work with most often:

| Token | Light default | Dark default | Role |
|-------|--------------|--------------|------|
| `primary` | `#9941FF` | `#B27BF2` | Main brand accent |
| `background` | `#F5F0FD` | `#26044D` | Page background |
| `surface` | `#FFFFFF` | `#000000` | Cards and panels |
| `text-primary` | `#9941FF` | `#B27BF2` | Primary text |
| `error` | `#E61D1D` | `#F75959` | Errors and destructive actions |
| `warning` | `#BE5702` | `#FA9F00` | Warnings |
| `success` | `#1C8731` | `#30A849` | Confirmations |
| `border` | `#E6E6E6` | `#333333` | Dividers and borders |

## Typography Size Reference

The `desktop` and `mobile` scales each define the same named tokens with different `font-size`/`line-height` pairs. Here's a subset you'll work with:

### Desktop scale (`typography.desktop`)

| Token | Font size | Line height | Font weight |
|-------|-----------|-------------|--------------|
| `headline` | `66px` | `88px` | `500` |
| `heading-1` | `40px` | `52px` | `500` |
| `heading-2` | `32px` | `44px` | `500` |
| `body-extended` | `16px` | `24px` | `400` |
| `body-base` | `14px` | `20px` | `400` |
| `caption` | `12px` | `16px` | `400` |

### Mobile scale (`typography.mobile`)

| Token | Font size | Line height | Font weight |
|-------|-----------|-------------|--------------|
| `headline` | `32px` | `40px` | `500` |
| `heading-1` | `28px` | `36px` | `500` |
| `heading-2` | `24px` | `32px` | `500` |
| `body-extended` | `16px` | `24px` | `400` |
| `body-base` | `14px` | `20px` | `400` |
| `caption` | `12px` | `16px` | `400` |

Next, you'll edit some of these color and typography tokens and regenerate the corresponding color assets in your local clone.

